<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canberra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canberra is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, built in the Australian Capital Territory as a compromise between the rival cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. The site was chosen in 1908 and named in 1913, and the city was planned by the American architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-burley-griffin">Walter Burley Griffin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marion-mahony-griffin">Marion Mahony Griffin</a> around an artificial lake. Parliament moved there from Melbourne in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canberra.jpg" alt="Canberra" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The 3B's · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_panorama_from_Mount_Ainslie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The plan for <b>Canberra</b> came from an international competition won in 1912 by the American architect Walter Burley Griffin, with drawings by his wife and partner Marion Mahony Griffin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…3,400, about a third of them black volunteers, organised as light infantry for counter-insurgency; the Royal Rhodesian Air Force had 1,000 personnel, Hawker Hunter and de Havilland Vampire strike aircraft and <b>Canberra</b> bombers; the British South Africa Police fielded paramilitary units and the Central Intelligence Organisation gathered intelligence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…rights movement; the Aboriginal Land Rights Act followed in 1976, the Aboriginal Tent Embassy was set up in <b>Canberra</b> in 1972 to demand sovereignty, and in 1992 the Mabo Case declared terra nullius invalid and recognised native title, which the Native Title Act of 1993 put into statute.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
